ewer, like baptism and communion. So, that's another difference.\n\nBut wait, some Protestant denominations might have more in common with other branches than others. For example, how do Lutherans differ from, say, Methodists? Are those considered part of Protestant or separate? I think they're still Protestant because they emerged from the Reformation. So, maybe the main differences between Protestant and Christian are more about the historical context and the Reformation, rather than theological differences?\n\nI'm getting a bit confused. Let me try to break it down. Christianity is the broader term, encompassing all denominations that trace their roots back to Jesus Christ. Protestantism is a specific branch that emerged after the 16th-century Reformation, which aimed to reform the Catholic Church. So, all Protestants are Christians, but not all Christians are Protestants. That makes sense.\n\nSo, the main differences would be historical, theological, and perhaps organizational. Historically, Protestants broke away from the Catholic Church, so that's a significant difference. Theologically, Protestants emphasize certain doctrines that came out of the Reformation, like justification by faith alone, as opposed to the Catholic view of justification through works. Organizationally, Protestant churches are generally more decentralized, without a single authority like the pope.\n\nBut I'm not entirely sure if all these points are considered the main differences. Maybe the most fundamental difference is the historical split during the Reformation, which led to the creation of Protestant denominations. Theological differences also play a role, as they have distinct doctrines. So, in summary, the main differences are historical (the Reformation), theological (doctrines like justification), and organizational (no single authority, decentralized structures).\n\nWait, but some people might argue that the theological differences are the main point. After all, the Reformation 