chings.\n\nI also need to consider the cultural and societal norms. In many Western cultures, including some Christian traditions, divorce is more common and seen as a way to move on when a marriage isn't working. But in Mormon culture, the emphasis on family and the sanctity of marriage might make members less likely to pursue divorce. Hindus, on the other hand, might have more lenient views, especially in certain regions or traditions.\n\nAnother angle is the concept of polygamy. Mormons are often associated with polygamous practices, especially historical ones, though the mainstream LDS Church now officially rejects polygamy. Hinduism, particularly in some communities, does allow for polygamous marriages, which might explain why the Hindu man could take a second wife without divorcing the first. The Mormon man might not be aware of this aspect of Hinduism and thus finds it surprising.\n\nThe fact that they all live together might also be a point of surprise. In Mormon culture, family is usually considered to be nuclear, with each couple living separately. Cohabitation, especially with multiple spouses, would go against their traditional family structures.\n\nIt's also possible that the Mormon man is surprised because he assumes that everyone follows the same strict marriage and family norms as he does. When he learns that the Hindu man has a different approach, perhaps one that he deems unconventional or even wrong, he reacts with surprise.\n\nI should also think about the theological differences. Mormons believe that families are eternal and that marriage is a necessary condition for exaltation in the afterlife. Divorce, therefore, is seen as a serious issue. Hindus, while having their own beliefs about the family and marriage, might not hold the same theological importance regarding marital status. This difference could make the Hindu man's actions seem unfamiliar or even shocking to the Mormon man.\n\nAdditionally, cultural taboos might play a role. In some cultures, including many where Mormons and Hindus live, remarriage after a divorce or taking multiple wives is not widely accepted. So even if the practices are allowed legally, socially they might still be seen as unconventional, which could explain the Mormon man's surprise.\n\nIn summary, the Mormon man is likely surprised because he expects that marital issues would be resolved through divorce before another marriage begins, he may not be aware of the Hindu practice of polygamy or cohabitation, or he could be reacting to the cultural and theological differences that allow for a different approach to family and marriage.\n<|im_end|>"}
