{"index": 1039, "question": "Who has one wife?", "ans0": "Undetermined", "ans1": "The Catholic man", "ans2": "The Mormon man", "context": "Both a Catholic man and a Mormon man were talking about their families. They had both been married twice, but the Catholic man was very surprised to learn that the Mormon one never divorced his first wife, he just took a second and they all live together.", "response": "Alright, so I'm trying to wrap my head around this situation where a Catholic man and a Mormon man are discussing their family lives. Both have been married twice, but the Catholic man is surprised to learn that the Mormon man didn't divorce his first wife\u2014he just took a second one and they all live together. Hmm, that's quite a unique arrangement. I guess the first thing I need to do is understand the religious perspectives on marriage and family that these two men hold.\n\nStarting with the Catholic perspective, I know that the Catholic Church teaches that marriage is a sacramental bond between a man and a woman, and it's considered a valid and indissoluble union until death. So, divorce isn't recognized by the Catholic Church. That must be why the Catholic man is surprised\u2014divorce isn't an option for him, so he probably assumes that the Mormon man would follow a similar practice. But the Mormon man took a second wife without divorcing the first, which, from the Catholic perspective, would seem like a situation where he's living in an illegal or condemned state.\n\nOn the other hand, the Mormon perspective on marriage is a bit different. I recall that the LDS Church (Latter-day Saints) also views marriage as a sacred and important institution, but I think they allow for divorce under certain circumstances, though it's not something taken lightly. They might have a process for annulment or separation, but certainly not the same as a typical divorce. So, if the Mormon man didn't divorce his first wife, maybe he considers her still his wife, and taking a 